From 5482e380fa3eeb8716d6151e20bda5a2950a2ab7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Zack Maril <zmaril@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 16:35:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] Add `-Zearly-metadata`: write metadata once item
 interfaces are checked

With `-Zearly-metadata`, the driver writes a second, early metadata file
(`libfoo-hash.early-rmeta`) after `analysis_interfaces`, before any
function body is type-checked, and announces it with an `early-metadata`
artifact notification. A build tool can start dependent crates on it
while this crate checks its bodies; a following commit lets rustc load
it. Full metadata is written as usual, later.

Early metadata holds what dependents need for analysis, not what they
need for code generation:

- Whatever the interface needs from a body is computed on demand: an
  opaque type's hidden type, the MIR of consts and const fns, coroutine
  witnesses. Statics are evaluated, and async closures' by-move bodies
  created, first, since encoding freezes the definitions table. Under
  `-Zthreads`, this runs in parallel like body checking.
- Definitions nested in other bodies (closures, inline consts, const
  arguments) are only encoded if their body is one the interface depends
  on (`interface_bodies`), a function of the crate's code alone.
- Optimized MIR (except coroutines'), deduced parameter attributes,
  exported symbols, the reachable set and the panic strategy are left out:
  computing them would check or optimize every body, and only code
  generation reads them.

Both files carry the same crate hash, computed once from the early
metadata's bytes plus the usual supplement (the HIR hash, covering every
body, and the tracked options), so it identifies the crate as completely
as before. Full metadata asserts that it agrees.

Before writing, the compilation takes an exclusive lock on
`libfoo-hash.rmeta.lock` and, if it links, `libfoo-hash.rlib.lock`, and
deletes stale full metadata and rlib. Each lock is released, and its file
removed, once that output is written, or when the compilation ends without
it. Dependents wait on these locks instead of polling. If the compilation
fails, the early metadata is deleted.

